> C does.  C++ has some more advanced features that aren't related to
> template metaprogramming, including inline functions, which seem to
> satisfy the requirements of your definition of "macro".

Inline functions *do not*.

One common use for Lisp macros is to define new syntax; since all Lisp
code can be described as a tree/list (Therefore its name: LISt
Processor), you can pass code as an argument to the macro. (Well, as a
matter of fact you can *only* pass code as an argument; it can't be
evaluated while the macro is running. Not with the expected results,
anyway.)

Also, the macro result is eventually evaluated in the same lexical
environment as the macro call, unless the macro takes special care to
avoid that. Inline functions aren't.

Here's a reasonably simple example that can't be done with an inline
function:

(defmacro setnil (symbols)
  (list 'progn
    (loop for s in symbols
     collecting `(setf ,s nil))))

This macro is used only for its side-effects; calling it as
(setnil '(a b c)) would result in the following code:

(progn
  (setf a nil)
  (setf b nil)
  (setf c nil))


(progn is a special form that evaluates all its arguments in left-to-right
order and returns the value of the last. It's often implicit.)

This can't possibly be done with an inline function, since this macro
*depdends* on being in the same lexical environment as the outside code;
it zeroes variables, after all.

